<!-- v2 (2026-11-15): rewritten for the rebuilt tool. The 1.x text described
     features that never existed (Identity Radar phases, a Wikipedia scraper,
     a VACUUM button). -->
<h3 class="help first">What this tool does</h3>
<p class="help">Database Tools lets you correct what the MetaForge database knows about your artists and albums, and remove records that shouldn't be there. It has three tabs.</p>

<h3>Artists</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Find an artist</strong> by typing part of the name and pressing Enter. If several match, choose one from the list.</li>
    <li><strong>Edit</strong> the name, country, biography and photo, then choose <strong>Save changes</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>A new name</strong> is carried through everywhere: the artist's albums, tracks and credits, and the artist tag in the music files. Before anything changes, MetaForge shows you how many albums, tracks, credits and files are affected and asks you to confirm. If another artist already has that name, the two are merged.</li>
    <li><strong>Merge with another artist</strong> when the same artist is in the database twice under two spellings (for example "The MG's" and "the M.G.'s"). Find the other record, choose which name to keep, check what changes, and confirm.</li>
    <li><strong>Albums</strong> lists every album the artist is on. Choose one to open it in the Albums tab.</li>
    <li><strong>Write a biography in Bio Builder</strong> opens Bio Builder with this artist already chosen.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Albums</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Details:</strong> title, album artist, release year, record label, the artist's country, genre and cover image. Title, album artist, year and label are also written to the music files' tags.</li>
    <li><strong>Genre:</strong> if the album's tracks have different genres, the list says "Tracks differ: leave them as they are" and saving leaves each track alone. Choose a genre only if every track should have it.</li>
    <li><strong>Tracks:</strong> edit each track's title and original year. On a compilation, each track also has its own artist. <strong>More…</strong> opens the track's mood, BPM, key, intensity and identifiers; leaving BPM or intensity blank keeps them blank.</li>
    <li><strong>Credits:</strong> the people who played on or made the album. Leave Tracks blank for the whole album, or list track numbers. <strong>Add credits from JSON</strong> adds a list of credits at once.</li>
    <li>Changes are kept only when you choose <strong>Save changes</strong>. "Unsaved changes" appears until you do, and MetaForge asks before you leave the tool.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Remove records</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Search for an artist, album, track or credit, choose one, and choose <strong>Remove the selected record</strong>.</li>
    <li>MetaForge shows exactly what will be deleted (with counts), and you type the word <strong>remove</strong> to confirm.</li>
    <li>Only database records are removed. <strong>Music files are never touched.</strong></li>
    <li>An artist who performs tracks on other artists' albums can't be removed; merge them into the right artist instead.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Backups</h3>
<p>Before any rename, merge or removal, MetaForge copies the database (and any MusicBrainz log it will update) into <code>%APPDATA%\MetaForge\backups\database_tools</code>, in a folder named with the date, time and what was done. The five most recent are kept.</p>
<!-- --- END OF FILE help.mfi --- -->
